Welcome to notebook six. The previous five notebooks built a vocabulary for values, behaviour, and data. This notebook gives you the tools Scala uses to package them together. Classes for instances with state. Objects for singletons. And traits for behaviour that can be mixed into multiple types. By the end of this session you will know how Scala expresses object oriented design, and how it adds a few twists on top of what java offers.

If you have written java, the shapes here will feel familiar. Class. Extends. Methods. Fields. The Scala twists are in the details. The primary constructor lives on the class line itself. Singletons are a first class language feature, not a pattern you simulate with private constructors. And traits can carry implementation in a way java interfaces only recently learned to do.

Let us start with the class. The class declaration in Scala 3 is unusually compact. The primary constructor is the class line itself. Parameters in parentheses after the class name become the constructor parameters, and they are available throughout the body of the class.

For example, a class called Point with two parameters, x and y, both of type Double, is written as, class Point, open paren, x colon Double comma y colon Double, close paren, colon, and then on the next line, indented, the body of the class. Inside the body you can write methods that refer to x and y. You construct an instance by simply calling the class name with the arguments. Point of three point zero and four point zero gives you a new Point. Notice there is no new keyword required. The bare Point of three and four works. You can still write new Point if you want to, but in idiomatic Scala 3 you do not need it.

Two important details. First, the parameters x and y in that example are visible inside the body of the class, but they are not public fields. They are constructor parameters, accessible to methods of the class, but invisible from outside. To turn a constructor parameter into a public field, you prefix it with val or var. Prefixing with val makes it a public read only field. Prefixing with var makes it a public read write field. With no prefix, the parameter is private to the class. The rule is the same one you learned for local bindings. Prefer val. Treat var fields like you treat var locals. Only when there is a clear reason for mutable state.

Second detail. The body of a class is a normal block expression. It can hold method definitions. It can hold value bindings. It can hold side effect statements that run at construction time, like printing a log line when the instance is created. Anything you can write in a block, you can write in a class body.

Methods inside a class are defined with def, the same as standalone methods from notebook three. The difference is that methods inside a class can refer to the constructor parameters and to other members of the class via the keyword this. You usually do not need to write this explicitly. Scala finds the member for you. But this is useful when there is an ambiguity, for example when a parameter has the same name as a field.

Visibility has three levels. Public, which is the default and means accessible from anywhere. Protected, which means accessible from this class and its subclasses. And private, which means accessible only from inside this class. There is also a refined form called private package, which scopes visibility to a specific package. That becomes useful in larger codebases when you want to share an implementation detail among files in the same package but not expose it as part of the public application programming interface.

Take a bank account class as an example. The constructor declares an owner, which is a public val, and a balance, which is a private var. The class exposes deposit and withdraw methods that mutate the balance internally. It exposes a currentBalance method that returns the balance read only. Code outside the class can call deposit, withdraw, or currentBalance. It cannot read or write the balance field directly. The private modifier protects the invariant that balance changes only go through the deposit and withdraw checks. Notice also the use of the require function inside the methods. Require throws an exception if its boolean argument is false. It is a standard precondition check that is built into the Scala standard library.

Now let us meet the second tool. The object. An object in Scala defines a single named instance that is created lazily the first time it is referenced. It has no constructor parameters because there is only one. It is Scala's replacement for java's static members and java's singleton pattern, rolled into one feature.

You use object for several purposes. Utility functions that have no per call state, like a math utility that exposes a circle area method. Application entry points, paired with the at sign main annotation from notebook one. Constants that do not belong to any specific instance. And companion objects, which is the most idiomatic use and deserves its own section.

When an object is defined in the same source file with the same name as a class, the two are called companions. The companion object can see the class's private members, and vice versa. The companion is the natural place for factory methods, constants tied to the type, and the special method called apply.

Apply is the key trick to understand. When you write some name in parentheses with arguments, like Temperature of twenty two point zero, Scala interprets that as Temperature dot apply of twenty two point zero. It calls a method called apply on the companion object. You almost never need to write apply yourself for ordinary classes, because Scala 3's auto generated constructors handle the common case. But the same apply mechanism powers some of the most idiomatic syntax in the language. When you write List of one comma two comma three, that is List dot apply on the List companion object. When you write Map of the alice arrow thirty pair, that is Map dot apply. Read every name in parentheses expression in Scala as, invoke apply on that name. That single mental rule explains a huge amount of the surface syntax.

A companion object can also expose factory methods with descriptive names. For example, a Temperature companion can expose a fromFahrenheit factory that converts the input from Fahrenheit to Celsius before constructing the temperature. It can also expose constants like absolute zero. The companion is where you put anything that conceptually belongs to the type but not to a specific instance.

Now the third tool. The trait. A trait in Scala is like an interface that can also carry implementation. Multiple traits can be mixed into a single class. A trait can declare abstract members. A trait can provide concrete methods. And a trait can hold its own state.

Take a simple Greeter trait that defines an abstract method called name and a concrete method called greet that uses name to build a greeting string. A class can declare it implements the trait with the extends keyword. If the trait has abstract members, the class must supply them, either as constructor parameters with the val prefix, or as method definitions. In Scala 3, traits can also take constructor parameters of their own, which is a new ability since Scala 2 did not allow that.

The reason traits matter so much is mixin composition. A class can extend many traits. The first trait is named with extends. Each additional trait is named with the keyword with in Scala 2 style, or with a comma in Scala 3 style. So in Scala 3 you can write, class Person, extends Greeter comma Logger comma Auditable, and the class picks up the behaviour of all three traits.

Mixin composition is how you build small, focused traits in Scala. A trait called hasName. A trait called Greeter. A trait called Logger. A trait called Cacheable. A trait called Auditable. Each trait stays cohesive. Each trait does one thing. And a class declaration shows exactly which behaviours apply to it, by listing the traits it mixes in. This is the idiomatic alternative to deep inheritance hierarchies. Instead of one huge base class with everything, you have many small traits combined as needed.

But what happens when two traits define the same method? Scala handles this with a process called linearization. Linearization takes the trait hierarchy of a class and orders it into a single line. When you call a method, Scala walks that line and uses the first concrete implementation it finds. For most cases this just works. Conflicts are rare in well designed traits. When you do hit a conflict, you can call a specific ancestor's implementation explicitly using super of the trait name, dot, the method name. We will revisit linearization in the type system notebook, number twelve, when it actually matters in practice. For now the rule of thumb is that the rightmost trait in your extends clause wins for method resolution conflicts. So the order of traits in the extends clause matters.

Now a question that comes up often. When should you pick an abstract class instead of a trait? Both can carry implementation. Both can have abstract members. So when do you pick one over the other? Pick abstract class when you need a single primary constructor with parameters and you are on Scala 2. Pick abstract class when you are interoperating with java code that expects a single base class. Pick abstract class when there is exactly one is a relationship and you want to forbid mixin composition. Otherwise, pick trait. In practice, for new Scala 3 code, default to trait. Reach for abstract class only when one of those rare reasons applies. In Scala 3, traits can take constructor parameters, which removes the most common historical reason for choosing abstract class over trait.

One last shape worth previewing. Extension methods. Scala 3 lets you add methods to an existing type from outside, using the keyword extension. The method then becomes callable as if it were defined on the type itself. For example, you can write an extension on String that adds a method called shout, which returns the uppercase version of the string with an exclamation mark appended. After that extension is in scope, you can call the string hello, dot shout, and get back the string capital h e l l o exclamation mark. Extension methods are how you enrich types you do not own. String. Int. Library classes. Without subclassing them. Notebook eleven covers them in depth alongside given and using clauses, because extensions and givens together form the type class pattern in Scala.

Let us close with a small model that uses all three pieces. A trait called Identified declares an abstract id member and provides a concrete describe method. A class called User extends Identified, providing the id as a constructor parameter prefixed with val, and overriding describe to give a more detailed string. A companion object for User exposes a constant called anonymous, which is a sentinel user, and a factory called fromEmail that constructs a User from an email address by taking the local part before the at sign as the id. The result is a tiny model with a trait providing default behaviour, a class providing instance state, and a companion providing convenience factories and constants.

Four things to notice in that closing example. First, the User class extends Identified and supplies the abstract id member through its constructor parameter. Second, the override of describe in the class uses the override keyword. The override keyword is required in Scala when you redefine a member that exists in a parent. The compiler enforces this, so you cannot accidentally shadow a parent method by typo. Third, the companion object exposes anonymous and fromEmail. Both are natural fits for a companion. They belong to the type, not to a specific instance. Fourth, the class and the companion share visibility. The companion can see the class's private members. This is occasionally useful for things like custom equals and hashCode methods.

You now have the imperative and object oriented shape of Scala. Notebook seven introduces the form that most real Scala code reaches for. Case classes, which are immutable data records with built in equals, hashCode, toString, and copy methods, automatically generated for you. And enums, which are Scala 3's tidy way to express finite sum types. Together case classes and enums form the algebraic data type backbone of Scala, and that backbone is what pattern matching in notebook eight will exploit.
